---
description: AUIES — show real component links for human choice, never fabricate a ranking score
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

# When more than one registry has a candidate

If registry search (per `02-registry-search.mdc`) turns up a plausible match
in more than one configured registry for the same plan item, do NOT invent a
score, percentage, or "best pick" to decide automatically. You cannot
measure animation quality, accessibility, or code quality by pattern-matching
component names — a number presented as if it were measured is misleading.

Instead:

1. List each candidate by name and registry.
2. Include its `docs_url` (or `demo_url` if present) from
   `registries/registries.json`, with `{name}` substituted for the actual
   component slug, so the user can open the real page and look at it.
3. Ask the user which one to install, or offer a one-line *factual*
   distinguishing note if you actually know one (e.g. "this one requires a
   Pro license key", "this one has no keyboard nav documented") — never a
   quality score.
4. Wait for the user's choice before installing. Do not default to the
   first result.

Example of correct behavior:

```
Found sidebar candidates:
- shadcn/ui — https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/components/sidebar
- @unlumen-ui — https://ui.unlumen.com/docs/components/sidebar (free tier)

Which would you like me to install?
```

# When only one registry has a candidate

Skip the link-comparison step — just say which registry it came from (per
`02-registry-search.mdc`) and install it. Don't manufacture a comparison
when there's nothing to compare.

# If a docs_url pattern looks wrong

`registries/registries.json` flags its `docs_url` values as unverified
placeholders. If a constructed link 404s or looks structurally wrong, say so
instead of presenting it as reliable, and fall back to naming the registry
and component without a broken link.
